# libqc Project

## Purpose

The libqc project is the core library for interacting with the P11 ERC-4337 smart wallet.

It is to be used to LibQC clients e.g. the Chrome Extension, iOS client etc.

Atlas is another product from P11 that provides ENS like functionality. They are smart contracts that provide functionality to register domain names and attach records.

## Core Libraries

- `viem` - Primary library for interacting with smart wallets
- `@zerodev/sdk` - Library for interacting with the ZeroDev implementation of the ERC-4337 wallet.
- `@noble/hashes` - Prefer this library for dealing with pre-quantum cryptography
- `@noble/post-quantum` - Prefer this library for dealing with post-quantum cryptography
- `@scure/bip32` - Always use this library for dealing with BIP32 derivation of private and public keys from indexes and mnemonics
- `@scure/bip39`- Always use this library for dealing with mnemonics

This project uses `pnpm` for Typescript dependency management (DO NOT USE `npm`, `yarn` or any other package manager).

## Glossary

- Wallet: A LibQC wallet is a collection of accounts
- Chain: A specific blockchain e.g. BASE, Ethereum, Solana
- Address: An address on chain. Can exist across multiple chains.
- Account: An address + chain pair in CAIP-10 format
- Mnemonic: A 24 word seed phrase
- Wallet Backup: The ability to recover a wallet e.g. via mnemonic import, PDF
- Validator: The contract that validates user operations in ERC-4337 e.g. ECC or ML-DSA validators
- Entrypoint: The ERC-4337 entrypoint that user operations are sent to
- User Operation: An ERC-4337 action that a user performs using their account e.g. Send a transaction, change a validator
- Bundler: An ERC-4337 bundler that bundles User Operations to send to the entrypoint
- Paymaster: An ERC-4337 paymaster that can sponsor gas for User Operations
- Factory: An ERC-4337 CREATE2 smart account generator
- Transaction: A settlement on-chain of either a native or ERC-20 contract call
- Atlas Domain Name: An ENS like domain name e.g. richard.q, conor.q, david.q
- Atlas Domain Name Registration: The action of registering a Domain Name
- Atlas Domain Name Resolution: The action of resolving a domain name (+ optional path) to a record
- Atlas Domain Name Record: Arbitrary data that can be resolved e.g. an account, address, color, email address

## Directory Structure

- `__tests__` - all Typescript tests
- `.github` - Github configuration e.g. workflows, PR templates etc
- `.husky` - Husky git hooks
- `src` - Contains all Typescript code and the core of the Typescript @project-eleven/libqc library
- `src/zeroDev` - Holds all logic for ZeroDev specific code. References to ZeroDev should NOT exist outside of this folder.

## General Design

- `src/vault.ts` - The exposed LibQC client library. Exposes an API that will be called by LibQC clients.
- `src/account.ts` - The account client. Created by `LibQC.getAccount`. Exposes functionality specific to accounts e.g. sending a transaction

## Commands

These are the commands that should be run when contributing to the project.

- `pnpm build` - Builds the library
- `pnpm test` - Runs tests
- `pnpm lint:check` - Checks ESLint linting
- `pnpm lint:fix` - Fixes ESLint issues where possible
- `pnpm prettier:check` - Checks for Prettier issues
- `pnpm prettier:fix` - Fixes Prettier issues

## General Guidelines

- All code must be tested accurately with well-designed unit tests
- ALWAYS run `pnpm prettier:fix` to fix prettier issues before finishing work
- ALWAYS run `pnpm lint:fix` to automatically fix ESLint linting issues before finishing work. Manually fix any remaining issues.
- ALWAYS run `pnpm test` to run the full test suit before finishing work. Scoping down tests (e.g. `pnpm test -- __tests__/unit/account.test.ts`) is acceptable when writing tests, but the full test suite MUST be run before yielding back to the user.
- All tests MUST pass for the work to be considered finished
- Always make sure that additional dependencies are well documented
